<!-- ===================== 01 ===================== -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The hidden-state explosion</div>
<h2>A tiny draft model needs a mountain of data</h2>
<p>Speculative decoding makes an LLM faster by pairing it with a small, cheap <strong>draft model</strong> that guesses several tokens ahead; the big <strong>target model</strong> then verifies those guesses in a single parallel pass. The modern recipe, EAGLE-3, doesn't draft from raw text — it drafts from the target's own internal <strong>hidden states</strong>. That makes the draft accurate, but it creates a brutal data-logistics problem: to train the draft, you must capture the target's hidden states for every token of every training sample.</p>
<p>Those tensors are huge. For an EAGLE-3 draft of Kimi K2.5, a <strong>single 128K-token sample is ~7 GB</strong>. The draft model itself is a few hundred MB — but feeding it requires a river of activations from a 1T-parameter target. Scale that to a real dataset and the storage is absurd.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">storage_explosion.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">pick a dataset size</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="cv-storage" height="280"></canvas>
    <div class="vp-controls" id="storage-ctrls">
      <button class="vp-btn" data-n="10000">10k samples</button>
      <button class="vp-btn active" data-n="100000">100k samples</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" data-n="600000">600k samples</button>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-caption" id="storage-cap"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The breakdown per 128K sample, in bf16:</p>
<table class="results-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Tensor</th><th>Shape</th><th>Size</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Hidden states (3 aux layers)</td><td class="mn">(131072, 21504)</td><td class="mn">5.25 GB</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Last hidden states</td><td class="mn">(131072, 7168)</td><td class="mn">1.75 GB</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Input IDs</td><td class="mn">(131072,)</td><td class="mn">1 MB</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Total per sample</strong></td><td class="mn">—</td><td class="mn">~7.0 GB</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>At 100k samples that's <strong>700 TB</strong>. This single number is the reason TorchSpec exists: the data you need to <em>move</em> dwarfs both models combined, so the way you move it <em>is</em> the system.</p>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 02 ===================== -->
<section id="s-eagle" data-code="eagle">
<div class="section-no">02 — What a draft model eats</div>
<h2>EAGLE-3: drafting from fused features</h2>
<p>To see why the hidden states matter, walk through one EAGLE-3 step. The draft model is a single transformer layer that reuses the target's embedding and LM head. What makes it good is <strong>training-time test</strong>: instead of predicting one top-layer feature, EAGLE-3 fuses <strong>low, mid, and high</strong> layers of the target into one rich feature vector, then drafts directly in token space. That's where the <code>(seq, 21504)</code> "3 aux layers" tensor comes from — 3 × 7168.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">eagle3_step.walkthrough</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">step through one draft+verify cycle</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="eagle-steps">
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Target runs, captures fused features</div>
          <div class="step-desc">For prompt "The cat sat on the…", the target forward pass exposes low/mid/high hidden states. These are concatenated into one feature per token.</div>
          <div class="step-code">h = concat(h_low, h_mid, h_high)  # (seq, 21504)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Draft proposes K tokens</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The 1-layer draft autoregresses from the fused feature, proposing a short tree: ["mat", "floor", "rug"].</div>
          <div class="step-code">draft.generate(h, k=3) → ["mat","floor","rug"]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Target verifies all K in one pass</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The target scores the whole proposed tree in a single parallel forward — the speedup comes from amortizing one expensive pass over many tokens.</div>
          <div class="step-code">logits = target(prompt + proposals)  # 1 pass</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Accept the longest valid prefix</div>
          <div class="step-desc">"mat" matches the target's own distribution → accepted. Output is identical to the target alone, but multiple tokens land per step. Lossless.</div>
          <div class="step-code">accept "mat" ✓  (3 tokens checked, ≥1 free)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="eagle-prev">‹ Prev</button>
      <button id="eagle-next">Next ›</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="eagle-prog"></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Training the draft means making step 2's proposals match step 4's acceptances as often as possible — which requires the <em>exact</em> fused features from step 1, for every token in the corpus. Hence the 7 GB/sample. The training problem is really a <strong>data-movement problem</strong>.</p>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 03 ===================== -->
<section id="s-approaches" data-code="approaches">
<div class="section-no">03 — Two ways that hit a wall</div>
<h2>Co-located vs. offline — and why both break</h2>
<p>Before TorchSpec, you had two options for getting those features to the trainer. Toggle between them — each hits a different wall at trillion-parameter scale.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">old_approaches.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">toggle the approach</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="cv-approach" height="320"></canvas>
    <div class="vp-controls" id="approach-ctrls">
      <button class="vp-btn active" data-a="colocated">Co-located (online)</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" data-a="offline">Offline dump</button>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-caption" id="approach-cap"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Co-located (online)</h3>
<p>Put the target and the draft trainer on the <em>same</em> GPUs. The target generates features and the draft trains in lockstep. The problem is memory and rigidity. Kimi K2.5 is a 1T-param MoE — about <strong>575 GB</strong> of weights. Shard it across 8 GPUs and each card carries ~72 GB of target:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>8×H200 (1,128 GB total):</strong> ~69 GB left per GPU for training</li>
  <li><strong>8×H100 (640 GB total):</strong> ~8 GB left per GPU — barely enough to train at all</li>
</ul>
<p>That tiny remainder caps context length at roughly <strong>4K tokens</strong>, and because the draft's parallelism is chained to the target's sharding, you can't grow past a single node. Rigid and starved.</p>

<h3>Offline dump</h3>
<p>Run the target once, write every feature to disk, then train separately. This decouples the two — but now you own <strong>700 TB</strong> of files, hammering a distributed filesystem with serialize/deserialize churn while multiple runs fight for I/O bandwidth. The trainer spends its life waiting on storage.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>The dilemma:</strong> co-located gives you no memory and no flexibility; offline gives you flexibility but an ocean of disk and an I/O bottleneck. TorchSpec's bet: keep the decoupling of offline, but never touch the disk.</div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 04 ===================== -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">04 — Disaggregation</div>
<h2>Two clusters, one network pipe</h2>
<p>TorchSpec splits the job into two independent fleets. An <strong>inference cluster</strong> runs vLLM or SGLang and does nothing but generate target hidden states. A separate <strong>training cluster</strong> runs FSDP and does nothing but optimize the draft. Between them, hidden states flow over the network through a transfer engine called <strong>Mooncake</strong> — never landing on disk.</p>

