<p>Three pieces make this work:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Worker Extension capture.</strong> On vLLM, TorchSpec uses the Worker Extension mechanism to hook the model forward pass and grab hidden states <em>inside</em> the worker process — no RPC serialization on the hot path. (On SGLang, a small patch enables the same extraction.)</li>
  <li><strong>Mooncake transfer.</strong> The captured tensors are handed to Mooncake, which moves them across the network via GPU-Direct RDMA (or TCP) with zero-copy, pre-registered pinned buffers.</li>
  <li><strong>Independent scaling.</strong> Because the two fleets share nothing but the pipe, you size them separately — more inference GPUs when feature generation is the bottleneck, more training GPUs when optimization is. The draft trainer gets the <em>full</em> GPU memory.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<!-- ===================== 05 ===================== -->
<section id="s-stream" data-code="stream">
<div class="section-no">05 — Streaming without disk</div>
<h2>The same data, but it never lands</h2>
<p>The whole win is one idea made physical: in the offline world, every feature tensor is written to a disk and read back; in TorchSpec, it flies straight from an inference GPU to a training GPU. Hit <strong>Play</strong> and toggle the path to watch the difference.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">hidden_state_flow.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Play · toggle disk vs. stream</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="cv-stream" height="300"></canvas>
    <div class="vp-controls" id="stream-ctrls">
      <button class="vp-btn" data-mode="disk">Offline (via disk)</button>
      <button class="vp-btn active" data-mode="stream">TorchSpec (stream)</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" id="stream-play" style="margin-left:auto;border-color:var(--purple);color:var(--purple-l)">▶ Play</button>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-caption" id="stream-cap"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Because the trainer no longer co-exists with a 575 GB target, its GPUs are free for activations — and that directly buys context length:</p>
<div class="stat-grid">
  <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">44K</div><div class="stat-label">max input tokens on H100 (full GPU memory for the draft)</div></div>
  <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">200K</div><div class="stat-label">max input tokens on B200</div></div>
  <div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">0 TB</div><div class="stat-label">disk used — features never materialize</div></div>
</div>
<p>Mooncake isn't a research toy: it's the same production transfer engine used in large-scale LLM serving, so the streaming path inherits zero-copy GPU-Direct RDMA and battle-tested reliability rather than a bespoke socket layer.</p>
</section>

